## 3. Glossary of Technical Terms

To help you gain full confidence in this environment, here is an absolute guide to the technical terms you will see throughout the codebase:

### 🌐 Next.js & React Concepts

#### App Router
Introduced in recent Next.js versions, this is the modern routing system. Files named `page.js` inside folders translate automatically to browser routes (e.g., `src/app/drimo/page.js` becomes `/drimo` in the URL).

#### Layouts (`layout.js`)
Shared UI shell that wraps surrounding pages. It prevents nested components from rebuilding when navigating between sibling routes (like clicking around the sidebar).

#### Route Handlers (`route.js`)
Custom request handlers that act as backend API endpoints. They process standard REST methods (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) and return JSON payloads.

#### Client Components (`"use client"`)
React components that execute in the browser. You must place `"use client"` at the top of files that utilize user interaction (like clicking a button, running timers, or triggering React hooks like `useState` and `useEffect`).

#### Server Components
By default in the App Router, files are compiled as Server Components. They fetch data directly from databases on the server-side, reducing client-side bundle sizes and speeding up initial page loads.

#### Next.js Middleware
Code that triggers *before* a request is fully processed. It is useful for inspecting request headers, managing logins, and enforcing security policies before serving HTML to a user.

---

### 🗄️ Database & Mongoose Concepts

#### MongoDB & NoSQL
A document-based database. Instead of writing rigid rows and columns (like SQL), it stores entries as dynamic JSON-like documents.

#### Mongoose
An Object Data Modeling (ODM) library for MongoDB and Node.js. It allows you to declare structured schemas (like validating that `email` is lowercase or `status` is limited to an enum).

#### DB Connection Caching
In serverless environments, Next.js routes fire in isolated execution threads. Mongoose caching (used in `src/lib/db/connect.js`) stores the database connection in a global variable so that consecutive API calls reuse the open pipeline instead of overwhelming the database with new connections.

#### Mongoose Population (`.populate()`)
A query technique that automatically replaces an ID field with the full document from another collection (e.g., converting `currentOrgId` inside a Driver document into the actual Organization object).

#### Enums
Rigid validation constraints ensuring a text field can only accept a specific list of strings (for example: `status: { type: String, enum: ['active', 'inactive', 'on_leave'] }`).

#### Database Indexes (`.index()`)
Performance flags created on columns. If we query driver records by their organization often, adding `DriverSchema.index({ currentOrgId: 1 })` speeds up search lookups.

---

### 🔐 Security & Auth Concepts

#### JSON Web Token (JWT)
An encoded string containing a secured data payload (like `{ userId: '123', role: 'super_admin' }`). Since it is cryptographically signed, the server can trust that the client has not tampered with their login state.

#### Scope vs Role
- **Scope**: General domain boundaries. In this app, scopes are either `drimo` (internal application) or `org` (client application).
- **Role**: Level of authority inside a scope. A user in the `drimo` scope might have the `accounts_admin` role, whereas a user in the `org` scope might have the `hr_manager` role.

#### Hashing & Salts (Bcrypt)
Security algorithm that converts plain-text passwords into encrypted strings (e.g., `password123` becomes `$2a$12$R9h/c...`). This is one-way encryption—it is virtually impossible to decode back to the original password, making the database secure if leaked.

---

### 🎨 Styling & Tailwind Concepts

#### Tailwind Directives (`@tailwind`)
Special tags in your CSS files (`@tailwind base; @tailwind components; @tailwind utilities;`) that instruct Tailwind's compiler to inject its pre-made responsive classes.

#### `@apply`
A Tailwind helper directive that allows you to pack multiple utility classes into a single, clean CSS class (e.g., combining `.badge-green { @apply bg-emerald-500/15 text-emerald-400; }`). This keeps components neat and avoids cluttering React files with hundreds of inline CSS tags.

#### CSS Variables (`:root`)
Global dynamic styling tokens (e.g., `--drimo-bg: #0F172A`). They allow you to shift whole interface accents dynamically by altering key color values inside a central file.

